// MiniGalaxy_Zoom — two LilyGO T-QT Pro boards.
//
// A slow "powers of ten" ride: the view starts on a chunky-pixel Earth and keeps zooming
// out. The Moon swings into view, then the Sun takes the centre and the planets circle it,
// then the Oort cloud, the nearest stars, and finally the whole Milky Way with a dot for
// "you are here". Then it fades to black and starts again. One cycle takes ZOOM_MINUTES.
//
// Both boards run the same cycle in sync (shared clock), each showing a different side of
// the Earth. Bring the boards together at ANY point in the cycle and whatever is on screen
// is torn apart and spirals into ONE black hole sitting in the gap between the screens,
// exactly like MiniGalaxy_Earth. Everything drawn is a particle, so the merge
// works the same for Earth dots, planets, stars and galaxy dots.
//
// The zoom skips quickly through the empty stretches (the dark between Neptune and the
// nearest star) and lingers where there's something to see. Simulated time speeds up with
// the zoom, so the Moon and planets visibly move along their orbits.
//
// How it works: both boards broadcast a small ESP-NOW packet ~40x per second (no router,
// no Wi-Fi network). Each board uses the other's signal strength (RSSI) as a rough distance
// sensor. The packets also carry a shared clock and each board's proximity value.
//
// Flash the SAME sketch to both boards. See README.md for Arduino IDE settings.
// To preview on the PC without flashing: python sim/preview_zoom.py
//
// Buttons:
//   KEY  (right button, IO47) : swap which board is "left" (flips BOTH boards)
//   BOOT (left button,  IO0)  : toggle the debug overlay (signal strength, frames per second
//                               in blue, zoom level in orange, L/R, proximity bar)

